window.docContents[119].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nAlgebraic graph theory studies a graph via algebraic objects attached to it, above\nall its automorphism group, which measures how symmetric the graph is, and its\nadjacency matrix, whose powers count walks. The two interact: a permutation of the\nvertices is an automorphism iff it preserves the adjacency matrix.\n\nIn this chapter we start from Mathlib's group structure on the automorphisms G ≃g G\nof a graph, show that isomorphic graphs have isomorphic automorphism groups, and\ncompute the automorphism groups of the infinite path pathNat₁ (trivial) and of the\ncycle graph C[n] (dihedral). We then introduce the adjacency matrix, and show that\nevery vertex of a vertex-transitive graph lies on the same number of closed walks of\neach length, and that isomorphic graphs have the same eigenvalues, though not\nconversely. We also find eigenvalues of Mycielskians, where the golden ratio appears.\nWe survey Mathlib's results on the Laplacian, whose kernel has dimension equal to the\nnumber of connected components, and on strongly regular graphs such as the Petersen\ngraph. We then show that the Hamming cube model hypercubeHamming of the hypercube\nis isomorphic to our recursive box product construction.\n\nThe final three sections are more advanced. Using the Hamming cube model of Q[n],\nwe prove that its automorphism group is the hyperoctahedral group ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, of\norder 2 ^ n * n!. We then count copies of one graph in another: the unlabelled\ncopies of K[s] are the s-cliques, and in general the number of labelled copies is\nthe number of unlabelled copies times the number of automorphisms, which we apply to\ncycles. Finally we show that every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is an induced subcube,\nand count these copies.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"12. Algebraic Graph Theory","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks":{"contents":"* pathNat₁.path: walk on the infinite path from i to i + j.* pathGraph.path: walk along the finite path graph P[n + 1] from 0 to\nFin.last n.* HypercubeVert.hammingPath: path between two hypercube vertices.* HypercubeVert.hammingDist: Hamming distance between two hypercube vertices\n(number of differing coordinates).* HypercubeVert.grayStart / HypercubeVert.grayEnd: start and end vertices of the\nGray code walk.* hypercube.grayPath: Gray code walk on Q[n].* Walk.mycielskianAux: auxiliary zig-zag walk in mμ[G].* Walk.mycielskian: walk in mμ[G] constructed from a walk in G (protected, to\ncoexist with the graph constructor mycielskian).* Walk.mycielskianClosed: closed walk in mμ[G] constructed from a closed walk in\nG (a cycle if the original is one).* Walk.posaRotate: Pósa rotation of a path walk.* Walk.posaClosed: closing a Pósa path to obtain a cycle.* Walk.IsMaximalPath: a maximal path — a path that cannot be extended at either\nend, i.e. every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it.* IsOrePair: Ore's condition at a pair of vertices — if distinct and non-adjacent,\ntheir degree sum is at least |V|.* IsClosed: a closed graph — one with no eligible pair, i.e. any two distinct\nvertices with degree sum at least |V| are adjacent.* closureStep: one round of the closure process — join every eligible pair at once.* closureAux: fuel-limited iteration of closureStep.* closure: the Bondy–Chvátal closure of a graph (computable).* IsOreGraph: an Ore graph — every pair of vertices satisfies Ore's condition\nIsOrePair.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* hypercube.grayCycle: Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2].* chordedC5: C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3, a graph that is not\nan Ore graph but whose closure is complete.* Walk.IsMaximumPath: a maximum-length path — a path such that no path in G is\nlonger.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"Chapter 8: Walks","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks"}});